==English==
{{wikipedia|pharmacy (disambiguation)|pharmacy}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|pharmacy}}, borrowed from {{der|en|frm|pharmacie||the [[art]] of [[creating]] [[drug]]s; a [[drug]], [[especially]] a [[laxative]]}}, from {{der|en|fro|farmacie}}, from {{der|en|ML.|pharmacia}}, from {{der|en|grc|φαρμακεία||the use of drugs}}, from {{m|grc|φάρμακον||a drug, charm, enchantment}}, of uncertain but likely {{der|en|pregrc|-}} origin. Attested since late 14th century.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{enPR|fär'məsē}} {{IPA|en|/ˈfɑɹməsi/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈfɑːməsi/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-pharmacy.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-pharmacy.ogg|Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{label|en|countable}} A [[place]] [[where]] [[prescription drug]]s [[are]] [[sold]] or [[dispensed]].
# {{label|en|uncountable}} The [[science]] of [[medicinal]] [[substance]]s, [[inclusive]] of [[pharmaceutics]], [[pharmaceutical]] [[chemistry]], [[pharmacology]], [[phytochemistry]], and [[forensics]].
# {{label|en|uncountable}} The [[role]] or [[occupation]] of a [[pharmacist]].

====Usage notes====
The American {{m|en|drugstore}} and British and Commonwealth {{m|en|chemist's}} are more common when referencing a small shop, especially when it is run as a general store that sells food and other goods as well as medicines. The pharmacies operated within hospitals that dispense medicine without acting as a point of sale are sometimes distinguished as {{m|en|dispensaries}}.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|drugstore}} {{l|en|drugstore}} {{q|US}}; {{l|en|chemist}}, {{l|en|chemist's}}, {{l|en|chemist's shop}} {{q|UK}}; {{l|en|dispensary}} {{q|dated}}; {{l|en|druggist's}}, {{l|en|druggist's shop}} {{q|US dated}}; {{l|en|apothecary's}}, {{l|en|apothecary's shop}} {{q|UK dated}}; {{l|en|apothecary}} {{q|historical or archaic}}

